#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157171 :question/id #uuid "f1a4b9b1-a6fd-504b-882f-df32959c35ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7838" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1}-16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-16){3}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3},$ and their product is $\\left( -\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "9a307c1f-ecef-5a41-99c7-d97e38fc1f53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154596} {:answer-field/id #uuid "f4c86233-fc69-5709-aa26-6ecdc11cb602" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186154597}] :question/id #uuid "556ae9cc-58db-5aae-8b6b-3fad8cb4fd16" :question/math-academy-id "q-237949" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $3x^{2} \\cdot e^{x + 5} - 2 \\cdot e^{x + 5} = 5x \\cdot e^{x + 5}$ are$x =$\n{{field-1}} and $x =$\n{{field-2}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b43031-2c1c-59db-af71-4eb4ae08bd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156676} {:answer/id #uuid "be3a38b8-2721-5192-a7d7-1631d8e2ec75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156677} {:answer/id #uuid "e7221e11-3e21-59f9-8c1b-404d52c225b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156678} {:answer/id #uuid "78657c4e-4d87-503c-8dd3-e41bf0dfe5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156679} {:answer/id #uuid "78a10047-013f-5ebe-840f-7ccfebbae9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156680}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bf427dca-9588-5c20-a1a2-8b86ed5da176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156675}] :question/id #uuid "23dd16f1-bbbd-5da8-a565-712015ada7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-88417" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4x^{2}e^{x} = 25e^{x}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234470 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f70c750-2bdb-5272-875c-7d304eb180d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-88472" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}=3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+4x-3){e}^{5x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{5x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(2x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{5x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{5x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},$ and their product is $(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "482b02ae-d24d-54b9-bec3-9abb19ad3a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236281} {:answer/id #uuid "5109d123-8dc4-51ab-ab40-bc9ecd7d988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236334} {:answer/id #uuid "94ecd205-8acf-5dfb-8b92-028d364421e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236761} {:answer/id #uuid "a7056fd7-4693-5e50-92d2-77473a227f45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236863} {:answer/id #uuid "f1cbddf2-0764-540e-8959-a9fe3253a384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5109d123-8dc4-51ab-ab40-bc9ecd7d988e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236334} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b561d1f-b212-5afc-9626-cc8d7995672a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39d52e4f-2b82-55ff-af5e-28f93f87917e" :question/math-academy-id "q-100797" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}=13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x} & =13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}-13x\\cdot {8}^{x}-6\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-13x-6)\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(5x+2)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3.$\n\n- The equation ${8}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${8}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot 3=-\\frac{6}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e24fbc0-aab2-5aed-8f87-83e63dcd458f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e24fbc0-aab2-5aed-8f87-83e63dcd458f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291104} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdf820c1-3999-5a03-a5c2-b46691647cde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292356} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f197341a-46a2-5a13-8785-e112d311489d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f197341a-46a2-5a13-8785-e112d311489d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291648} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7e40b27-2945-566a-ac76-7521545ce25f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c61f15b5-8695-5a43-8d54-6ddb2e0a3852" :question/math-academy-id "q-264786" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}=50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2x-5}-50\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-50)\\cdot {3}^{2x-5} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-50=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{3}^{2x-5}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-50=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-25) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+5)(x-5) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-5,5.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${3}^{2x-5}=0$ has no solutions because ${3}^{2x-5}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-5\\,\\text{and}\\,x=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292664 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e00ad2a-0346-5764-8bd0-cbcc0f4a797a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e00ad2a-0346-5764-8bd0-cbcc0f4a797a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291167} :answer-field/id #uuid "4304fe74-f9d5-5270-981d-8bec5b17751e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61ad1b3-442b-57c6-94cb-530214be2c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c61ad1b3-442b-57c6-94cb-530214be2c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291485} :answer-field/id #uuid "db84ff3f-88d9-57d9-80ab-3958548a8d86" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292416}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4930566b-e427-5630-b850-4beaebc0bb52" :question/math-academy-id "q-264787" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}=144\\cdot {5}^{x-4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}-144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-144)\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-144=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-4}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-144=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ 4(x+6)(x-6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation $4=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-4}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-4}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d3b4c08-4089-59bf-b334-460e863d844c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d3b4c08-4089-59bf-b334-460e863d844c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290962} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a67150-9c00-52e5-b264-43c659232781" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3b82486-25ce-5f32-92e1-f82f1823cd92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3b82486-25ce-5f32-92e1-f82f1823cd92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291525} :answer-field/id #uuid "080ca1cd-b991-539d-863f-a2bb99b34bce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291762}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42249fad-6fbc-52a8-a849-f0171242c90d" :question/math-academy-id "q-264788" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3}=64\\cdot {e}^{x+3}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =64\\cdot {e}^{x+3} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+3}-64\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-64)\\cdot {e}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}-64=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9{x}^{2}-64=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+8)(3x-8) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{8}{3},\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a62afea-1ad9-5b81-af91-5cfc260c46e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a62afea-1ad9-5b81-af91-5cfc260c46e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290947} :answer-field/id #uuid "84f5ec96-4b96-5348-bada-3ec722a4e046" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d32d22a3-9ff4-5d0d-a60c-9cc4c98f5e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d32d22a3-9ff4-5d0d-a60c-9cc4c98f5e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291524} :answer-field/id #uuid "f13a2d3e-e3c5-5420-9e0d-7cec21c92eea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292485}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "521ef3f1-5e52-5337-8f02-d3baee467302" :question/math-academy-id "q-264791" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x}=-11x\\cdot {7}^{x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x} & =-11x\\cdot {7}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+11x\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x}+ & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+11x+6)\\cdot {7}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+11x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+11x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+2)(x+3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e58a82f3-7ab9-5cd6-a371-47dce5022143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e58a82f3-7ab9-5cd6-a371-47dce5022143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291594} :answer-field/id #uuid "057ca71b-bfc0-5e67-9e71-aefb4febb0c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a76338-9337-5979-aac4-a167deb07009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42a76338-9337-5979-aac4-a167deb07009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290907} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4e3aa3-5b78-54df-ae1e-c1d2e05aa121" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292170}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "031413b5-b317-5f59-a8c7-fe15ae3f011c" :question/math-academy-id "q-264793" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x}=25x\\cdot {e}^{2x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =25x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}-25x\\cdot {e}^{2x}+6\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-25x+6)\\cdot {e}^{2x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-25x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{2x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-25x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(4x-1)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{4},x=6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{2x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{2x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c919c98-aec6-5ea1-88f6-1a91416e1eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351823} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee6ecb0-12c1-5332-882d-87551f016d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351998} {:answer/id #uuid "2f14a03a-22a6-5b44-abb3-dd8d62e01fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351999} {:answer/id #uuid "991574d0-b777-5061-9c7e-432b019b2d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352881} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4fc39d-08ba-5601-b435-eeaa16fe08d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "991574d0-b777-5061-9c7e-432b019b2d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352881} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a4488b-4b88-53e2-8c9b-78ea63e827aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc805550-3caa-5380-82bc-9ef272d45b26" :question/math-academy-id "q-88473" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}=7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}+4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-7\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+4x-7)\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+7)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot 1=-\\frac{7}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d960871-da12-5e60-90cc-73ecfc0215e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351830} {:answer/id #uuid "48175fbc-4f29-5607-8c6e-dc23662e3bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352232} {:answer/id #uuid "5c26fff7-1840-5c1a-8e8b-a1f23f55f47d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352381} {:answer/id #uuid "7dacc9d6-2bcf-5d03-8fed-5f9fb106fad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352671} {:answer/id #uuid "df8c2fb7-de0d-54ad-87aa-e9e11f817239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df8c2fb7-de0d-54ad-87aa-e9e11f817239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353508} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c0ec4a-c1e8-51a4-a783-48519801b33b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353944}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23aad5f9-739e-5764-b74b-f1c4da81d658" :question/math-academy-id "q-88474" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x}=6x\\cdot {5}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =6x\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-6x\\cdot {5}^{x}-45\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x-45)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-6x-45=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+3)(x-5)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $3=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3)\\cdot 5=-15.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83cca658-4075-5016-8fbe-e5612324db1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386580} {:answer/id #uuid "bb580306-8e14-5c41-83ea-3c1ee2e8cdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386581} {:answer/id #uuid "c11a3bed-2998-59a1-97a6-f48556210e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3957c19-6d34-5546-aa5e-4ed5649688c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386583} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddd09f0-bf49-52e2-98a4-7a4a058d7c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3957c19-6d34-5546-aa5e-4ed5649688c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386583} :answer-field/id #uuid "e20ce4da-f186-5091-a95d-1d090861a47d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386579}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ddd6b32-1ad1-5a92-b6ca-5f243dfcc079" :question/math-academy-id "q-110803" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{10}^{2-x}={10}^{2-x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{10}^{2-x} & ={10}^{2-x} \\\\ {x}^{2}{10}^{2-x}-{10}^{2-x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-1){10}^{2-x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-1=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{10}^{2-x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-1=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-1,1.$\n\n- The equation ${10}^{2-x}=0$ has no solutions because ${10}^{2-x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-1,1,$ and their product is $(-1)\\cdot 1=-1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707 :topic/math-academy-id 1929 :topic/title "Introduction to Partial Derivatives"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837ffa1c-bea1-5c32-a487-a5e0c1fa8464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3t + 2t^{4})e^{t^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165166} {:answer/id #uuid "f892b757-7eae-5c63-89c8-63741963fd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2t + 3t^{4})e^{t^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165167} {:answer/id #uuid "11856d3c-08b0-5dec-a86d-009e03dc99e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 + 3t^{3})e^{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165168} {:answer/id #uuid "198e92a0-3964-5d71-ae13-52081c329e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2 + t^{2})e^{t^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165169} {:answer/id #uuid "761d1b61-36d9-5b0f-b98f-40748d738557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3t + 2t^{3})e^{t^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165170}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b9b1652d-93ab-5313-9296-f0aa0f63c08f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165165}] :question/id #uuid "284d40a1-d125-53c0-b06b-70dfdac633d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94587" :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = xe^{xy}, x = t^{2}$, and $y = t$, compute $\\frac{df}{dt}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa7cb58-130b-52e2-9ffc-cc9e52330663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358818} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a9cb0c-c43b-5a83-8c67-e7c35f7eae70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359161} {:answer/id #uuid "ea13c98f-b638-5d97-8167-fa566662cb6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359835} {:answer/id #uuid "ec49dc46-0ba5-5fda-b9c2-6bec956ac528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359856} {:answer/id #uuid "f7162188-93f3-50e1-8684-87bec1a6cf00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec49dc46-0ba5-5fda-b9c2-6bec956ac528" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359856} :answer-field/id #uuid "a955c8c5-959c-5957-86ce-5b5f4f04ba90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360295}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19a64e51-db17-58fb-852b-340494362842" :question/math-academy-id "q-95078" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}),x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$ for $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284666 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "546085f7-b447-560f-8e03-5f9f83d6f2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282406} {:answer/id #uuid "74f33be1-f6b9-527c-9c36-b38596ce0f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282688} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6ac22c-45ee-5e02-aa23-fa2d93a62a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282915} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8a9385-1ddd-50d1-9fa6-1227b2a8f619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282916} {:answer/id #uuid "91107c67-4b51-563a-9b9f-7ec21682f202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d6ac22c-45ee-5e02-aa23-fa2d93a62a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282915} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0740db-ef2c-573e-a13f-6da541dd04e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284317}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f247252-c196-59cb-b56b-5c53685b26d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-170668" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x={t}^{2},$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot 2t+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0906d5df-95ee-50f2-a42b-09e69555e339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t(1+2t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357833} {:answer/id #uuid "140ea72d-9a0d-57b5-b67c-2049bb317343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357925} {:answer/id #uuid "9cea513d-612f-5290-b731-384d792ca237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359124} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ba0846-4e92-5a2d-b302-7d756aa55faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{t}^{3}(2+t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359440} {:answer/id #uuid "e95d1d3f-a370-59d0-97ce-9062ff9fd3d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t(2+t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cea513d-612f-5290-b731-384d792ca237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359124} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9bfd21b-4c44-5c5c-ba56-5fadb4ae0273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360377}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d8c80e5-aa7d-56a9-83f4-b1c7ab4b5caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95076" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y},x=2t$ and $y={t}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2t){e}^{{t}^{2}}=4t{e}^{{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(2t)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}=4{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+4{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =8t{e}^{{t}^{2}}+8{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1830bf59-5084-598f-b1f6-81191dfbfbcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+t}{\\sqrt{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357965} {:answer/id #uuid "26a1cc25-ba10-5462-a7f5-4b6c422818d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358100} {:answer/id #uuid "72ac5140-e75a-5b93-834e-7afad4225743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358751} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdd033a-0457-5b60-bc47-43fcb08320c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358978} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddb28d7-a49c-5dad-b528-bbf182a53bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ddb28d7-a49c-5dad-b528-bbf182a53bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358988} :answer-field/id #uuid "146b2117-94cd-5963-92fa-afe7f73a78f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360033}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0f22ddd-635a-5638-92e4-fbad8751067f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95077" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2xy},x=t$ and $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "181e483e-0db4-5bd1-964b-afa6fc2ecf27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357963} {:answer/id #uuid "719dcb03-d975-5054-b48a-74dd57cccf20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358745} {:answer/id #uuid "76fada7e-bbdf-508e-9127-190df8ac22e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358776} {:answer/id #uuid "81dec9c1-74ca-5c5c-9366-b27ffa80a962" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358886} {:answer/id #uuid "c17c2336-706c-5cd5-8b88-2ef24b3288c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "719dcb03-d975-5054-b48a-74dd57cccf20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358745} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee046ec2-08f8-5c58-bfb3-059a2ba841f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360423}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bb11d2f-2294-5c9e-b733-36535d04471b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95079" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x=4t$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4+(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5774d438-8549-5c81-9843-45d115052cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358513} {:answer/id #uuid "63dc5334-1342-5ac0-8adc-34168c5ef7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(s+2){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358624} {:answer/id #uuid "97245f20-2357-5a46-9419-5584f26e4952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(s+1){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359074} {:answer/id #uuid "bc07b503-6a18-5c74-b532-0d144efdccab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(s+2){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359386} {:answer/id #uuid "c201f1bf-9034-567f-9849-2029c4f2719a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{s}^{2}{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97245f20-2357-5a46-9419-5584f26e4952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(s+1){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359074} :answer-field/id #uuid "1991f021-d4bd-5ba5-9a63-d5673c4d3078" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40900f6f-b4af-5c59-b5c2-386989d530fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-95080" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={e}^{x+2y},x={s}^{2},$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & ={e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}\\cdot 2s+2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2s{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}+2{e}^{{s}^{2}+2s} \\\\  & =2(s+1){e}^{{s}^{2}+2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f944b8-e799-5036-80f1-5ba5c1a7411f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358364} {:answer/id #uuid "82479d60-5437-50e5-882f-1cefac414e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358893} {:answer/id #uuid "f1724fb0-eb83-58dc-8a3f-92689c7862c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359901} {:answer/id #uuid "fe84e3ca-9757-54b8-a1da-085a92705bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359990} {:answer/id #uuid "fede68b1-836b-57e9-a83f-57bb56eb0c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82479d60-5437-50e5-882f-1cefac414e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358893} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef608987-b651-5ccb-b9c8-ffdc510994e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360424}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0e7724b-5745-517d-9a70-23d66bdba953" :question/math-academy-id "q-95081" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x=s$ and $y=2s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=1+\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =1+\\frac{s}{2s}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s\\cdot 1+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b80e388-89f0-55d3-9647-29eb1e7d21a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357986} {:answer/id #uuid "2931a144-34bf-5b91-9fd0-2c8561636d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2s+{csc}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358129} {:answer/id #uuid "5f982da7-fadd-5a18-83b3-b5af485d42fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358586} {:answer/id #uuid "981660b8-6d8e-5d6b-a353-cc9e60ac0aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359084} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b01e60-3c8a-5b80-ab36-ad8a68a5308f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b80e388-89f0-55d3-9647-29eb1e7d21a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357986} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0a50c8-6fe1-52e2-992d-16da1ca6ce29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360348}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f22cdf5c-424c-5c09-91c4-a4b6bd7748c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95084" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y},x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s)+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s} \\\\  & =-2-2{cot}^{2}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdc706a-1707-5c09-affb-248453b8b3de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+{s}^{2}}{1+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357857} {:answer/id #uuid "154db3be-7d69-5ebc-979b-f406345e0c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s(1-s)}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357939} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc9a21b-0849-5652-a19d-cd8b9c45a0a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}(2+s)}{(1+s)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358001} {:answer/id #uuid "7cec9aba-8f27-55a0-917c-38e83d5f0aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358838} {:answer/id #uuid "e28edfdb-62cd-569f-b9f0-d31cb58213f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e28edfdb-62cd-569f-b9f0-d31cb58213f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359756} :answer-field/id #uuid "083d442a-f627-5528-9841-283282543ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360012}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a57f510-aa58-5248-9a92-0fa5039eed64" :question/math-academy-id "q-95085" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+y},$ $x={s}^{2}$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{1+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{(1+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{1+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+s}\\cdot 2s-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2s}{1+s}-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s(1+s)-{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383607 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97cfef0b-b2e9-52d1-81b0-399192df2eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383609} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd4c4de-e1e5-5ff6-83aa-88f43410589b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383610} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d046f0-8479-5ddd-8e29-9cf04c57ccd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383611} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf412ac-5ff2-5717-972e-75f25275ddc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383612} {:answer/id #uuid "9e75a459-147f-5daa-8fd7-55841122ff55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bd4c4de-e1e5-5ff6-83aa-88f43410589b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383610} :answer-field/id #uuid "614acb07-1733-5745-addd-2f741b4bd7c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5274ee67-b6e6-57da-a682-d2ddd4bf1256" :question/math-academy-id "q-170669" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={e}^{2y-x},x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{e}^{2y-x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{e}^{2y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247ee7d0-904c-5d01-8297-06a98015037c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{s}}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385840} {:answer/id #uuid "7cec0dc1-33bb-5f04-a034-05527df9b19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+s){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385841} {:answer/id #uuid "323b91bd-668a-507d-8f83-8a04b4f2b115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2+\\sqrt{s}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385842} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd6bb8a-7411-5765-b787-90052e6b40f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385843} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbb05f2-b967-534b-9feb-494c22d63500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fbb05f2-b967-534b-9feb-494c22d63500" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385844} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f70704d-2718-514e-b670-1b5ed75416ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385839}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c583feca-7024-59b3-b46e-988493997541" :question/math-academy-id "q-95083" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ $x=\\sqrt{s}$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}$ for $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =s{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =s{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{s}} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166586 :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00add8e7-6980-56f0-9a2a-bb552536c12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357760} {:answer/id #uuid "286d61f8-a3ff-5fbf-b774-2c2cb79b67bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358115} {:answer/id #uuid "6d859cb3-66c2-535d-8f19-f1b8830867ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358708} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe84aab-93e0-5d36-b349-3a91b328cae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359385} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a2ec28-c873-5c71-8848-26eba34233c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbe84aab-93e0-5d36-b349-3a91b328cae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359385} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aac33cc-3f0e-5b51-8653-40c739dd5910" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360038}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78ec1810-6288-5fe9-a1ed-f6d4d3a585e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94588" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{x}^{2})=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{x}^{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e272a6d-78d3-5cb9-95c9-c87f0fea3da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357869} {:answer/id #uuid "34692782-e7a3-51e2-939e-c7595c3e9b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358219} {:answer/id #uuid "69e98fd7-fe36-540b-bd06-de9d5d7bdda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})-\\frac{1+3{x}^{2}}{1+2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358677} {:answer/id #uuid "7b57f18a-b391-5d2a-a9af-d8d06a8a7f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-\\frac{1+2{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358823} {:answer/id #uuid "9695921f-e190-55c7-a45b-cceb0f8f0066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e272a6d-78d3-5cb9-95c9-c87f0fea3da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357869} :answer-field/id #uuid "000855cc-b4eb-5315-a2d3-71276d48abd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360003}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56630566-56de-5b10-a1f2-1989c1203d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-95121" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x+2{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x(1+2x)}{x(1+x)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021a846a-8825-55bf-a7b3-1dc5c5c5ff19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281709} {:answer/id #uuid "49b2d0a3-8640-5928-a7d2-f8a14102a68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a1e1c7-9e8a-5f17-9500-4d7507d44b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283715} {:answer/id #uuid "efd819c9-60d2-5826-9266-3dba4a49aaec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283779} {:answer/id #uuid "f5de75cd-c44e-5baf-9f07-d98f3333edab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49b2d0a3-8640-5928-a7d2-f8a14102a68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} :answer-field/id #uuid "d658fcd0-7d37-5a87-a5b6-42706ffef583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284307}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdba7da9-8893-51f6-a38d-eccc847b176c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170670" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "134e5ec5-1833-5763-a152-935445c16e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357915} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfec4cb-8120-5f44-9db6-9fe99f5be666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{e}^{-x}}{1+x{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357993} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd7195e-4163-5d9b-ab1f-f50c56f9bda4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358725} {:answer/id #uuid "a03e6cf2-e199-53c6-82f6-4585f233d777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359150} {:answer/id #uuid "e14a9608-4f8c-580c-b913-718d374bebe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{1+x{e}^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a03e6cf2-e199-53c6-82f6-4585f233d777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359150} :answer-field/id #uuid "69e0f45c-fd3c-57d2-96aa-73479d2efe99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360165}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d95d5a8a-ad5f-52dc-beb9-600c878518d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95088" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y}$ and $y={e}^{-x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}\\cdot (-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}+\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+{e}^{-x}}{(x+{e}^{-x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f7371e6-fa62-5258-b977-84ee4e88782e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358438} {:answer/id #uuid "67710e58-3007-513b-8494-0c76521975c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358654} {:answer/id #uuid "80e19a52-081f-5b37-8339-414407ee7d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358876} {:answer/id #uuid "901dd65d-6c65-58cd-87bb-517a683c4833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+3{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359008} {:answer/id #uuid "96f6c6ee-cd4d-5972-a765-20abbc025c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96f6c6ee-cd4d-5972-a765-20abbc025c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359072} :answer-field/id #uuid "d31946ed-c60d-5761-b68f-54a6782e1347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360357}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e7ed0c3-433c-5b76-94b3-8ee539ba166f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95114" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13df3da6-fa03-593a-b697-020ee592a4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357924} {:answer/id #uuid "205aa05e-a7f6-5abf-9311-2b838c211938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358036} {:answer/id #uuid "682e2303-0eea-58c6-9296-93c2403f441a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358663} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e70a37-4f39-508f-a9c2-c0d746a9d541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359443} {:answer/id #uuid "fa934368-527c-591b-bae3-d56a4c98c73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(1-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "682e2303-0eea-58c6-9296-93c2403f441a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358663} :answer-field/id #uuid "97ecada2-93d5-5ee3-a2ef-d0b7948dd606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360256}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "25aad332-4985-5005-bb5a-87ea911c6d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-95117" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be18679-efe0-59e6-80ac-db92f8d96a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9edd72-2523-57c6-9665-681f1ed475c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358439} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcdf56c-f98e-5632-8b37-69db930425d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359116} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b3c97-4572-526c-8afd-d0a1d5757af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359141} {:answer/id #uuid "b33efd16-e5a4-5396-8988-6df4afae8ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4be18679-efe0-59e6-80ac-db92f8d96a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f643ac2-8342-50cb-9136-e026a1d69059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfb1f74a-0323-5d9e-a2f6-877356ed98f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95118" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+yx$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dbcf33-c576-54cf-8c07-43b62cf69983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+4{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358069} {:answer/id #uuid "558df00d-6a5e-5bb3-bb8a-e8288e5b1e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1-4{x}^{3}}{x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358497} {:answer/id #uuid "67861fb7-92d5-5f5e-8f74-f8ff3c7f315c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-3{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358655} {:answer/id #uuid "70539894-ff4f-5b7d-809c-ae8c476c88fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358729} {:answer/id #uuid "da024232-5563-5f22-a044-1622a067232a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{x})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70539894-ff4f-5b7d-809c-ae8c476c88fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358729} :answer-field/id #uuid "f550e8b8-0dda-5ff3-ba5c-bfed41503899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360429}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f396a56-19fc-58e7-855c-d3a310fbb9ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-95119" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}))\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a85a62-20c8-53ea-9891-50994f57bf9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}(x-6)}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357811} {:answer/id #uuid "31bd0322-e103-58db-ab4e-d490d4079dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358194} {:answer/id #uuid "870209e6-e356-5edd-a169-1a95385cbde9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(3x+4)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358933} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ec2678-1f3d-53f9-9edd-d1c6fda28115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x(4x-3)}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359444} {:answer/id #uuid "d1925cff-23b1-5f65-a480-35f2ce80acbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1925cff-23b1-5f65-a480-35f2ce80acbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359584} :answer-field/id #uuid "a27a35b5-3297-5a09-b127-d1d744a50e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360275}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f411889e-b3c1-5aed-8247-b760065e6e98" :question/math-academy-id "q-95122" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y}{(x+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+1)-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16c2c5e9-da1d-55c7-bd83-1bd6b9ad79ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321334} {:answer/id #uuid "489a9808-ce22-5786-b985-9a14fb8a8b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "st{e}^{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321719} {:answer/id #uuid "697d6780-0b4f-5382-bd17-235155813022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{e}^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321974} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c25882-49c7-5840-b9c2-fc87b6311627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322436} {:answer/id #uuid "cff4cfea-c1b0-5d9d-9e19-ba1c7c52a29c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{e}^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cff4cfea-c1b0-5d9d-9e19-ba1c7c52a29c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{e}^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322769} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9293479-1c57-5ceb-a808-e4a28c33e7be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323531}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a13d6706-d1e1-54bc-9c0b-137475532529" :question/math-academy-id "q-37205" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2x+xy,$ where $x=s{e}^{s}$ and $y=t{e}^{s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2+t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =s{e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2+t{e}^{s})(0)+(s{e}^{s})({e}^{s}) \\\\  & =s{e}^{2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a19a158-cb30-5c9e-9cf9-891ab114ad36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(2t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355012} {:answer/id #uuid "392f1f1c-1dad-5e72-b1e4-40cf201a767c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}t(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355179} {:answer/id #uuid "869f5f73-0c65-5003-ade6-8eeef3e8b4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355855} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f0fe58-16c4-5d79-ab11-4893e9ff089a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8st(s-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356369} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ace28-41b6-5a2e-8b64-9dee698d22fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8st(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ace28-41b6-5a2e-8b64-9dee698d22fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8st(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853} :answer-field/id #uuid "6314b903-87e4-5c8c-a8db-703d520d008b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357085}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1e3cf4a-155f-587d-ba03-cc737759407e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94448" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y,$ where $x=2st$ and $y={s}^{2}t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2st)=4st, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(4st)(2t)+(-4)(2st) \\\\  & =8s{t}^{2}-8st \\\\  & =8st(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fa4d19-1a26-5b2d-9556-c54c686ac534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282582} {:answer/id #uuid "734e21fc-611a-5299-a426-c78cc36493b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282676} {:answer/id #uuid "778709fb-5770-565c-b6f9-6f6b5c95ff0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282710} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf8784d-5d98-5e83-8104-3b5974f0cfbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283327} {:answer/id #uuid "e535fa66-984a-5f50-aa43-6100959c6fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e535fa66-984a-5f50-aa43-6100959c6fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283689} :answer-field/id #uuid "13047c11-f65c-581c-a388-dd6cb0189624" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283972}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ab94495-9213-5241-a548-3a7deeaeec18" :question/math-academy-id "q-170672" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284669 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2954df91-e2e3-5363-9cf7-40eb665f51bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+s}(s+t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282047} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd2664d-1f59-5b44-bee4-99dfe3cb5c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{t+s}(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282074} {:answer/id #uuid "80097d83-68ae-522a-8521-7188df6108f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{t+s}(s+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282790} {:answer/id #uuid "8f85d891-1ebb-510b-809c-ca4e33750cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{e}^{t+s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282932} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f69daa-8b78-5912-b3b2-04861a1d8880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{e}^{t+s}(t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80097d83-68ae-522a-8521-7188df6108f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{e}^{t+s}(s+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282790} :answer-field/id #uuid "b11ef35f-87cc-5868-8479-09e0da99d040" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284242}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f91ae07-5e1e-548f-8551-8d2cfc3e050d" :question/math-academy-id "q-170673" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ where $x=t+s$ and $y=ts.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(ts{e}^{t+s})(1)+({e}^{t+s})(t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t+s}(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043fc35b-df2d-5e07-8cbe-03b7fa3823c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321189} {:answer/id #uuid "518cc1b5-633b-5f44-b41f-79ef11a5b400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321789} {:answer/id #uuid "704123a5-c11a-5ff9-85e0-48be32c71cd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{5}{t}^{2}+2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322021} {:answer/id #uuid "cbea4778-d1ae-52ee-b74f-3fd686a2645b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{3}{t}^{2}-4{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322731} {:answer/id #uuid "e322d4a9-1941-524c-a4fe-437235ac0079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-5{s}^{2}+2{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "518cc1b5-633b-5f44-b41f-79ef11a5b400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321789} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1da1c1b-1bb2-5f2d-969b-c27a582c5eaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323647}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f2a11e40-d8b5-5095-a64c-10947d9f3965" :question/math-academy-id "q-37138" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-2y,$ where $x=s{t}^{2}$ and $y={s}^{3}-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =({s}^{3}-t)^{2}={s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(s{t}^{2})({s}^{3}-t)-2=2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =({s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2})({t}^{2})+(2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2)(3{s}^{2}) \\\\  & ={s}^{6}{t}^{2}-2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{4}+6{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2} \\\\  & =7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d395ff-c9b8-57ab-ace3-d37966cc5235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t{e}^{-st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357822} {:answer/id #uuid "146dd4ab-8bac-5175-8723-3891d22237e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t{e}^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357927} {:answer/id #uuid "4962113f-d78c-5fe7-ae12-fd3b934aa764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358381} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd19d0f-60a8-5109-bf7a-caf1ec7c5da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358589} {:answer/id #uuid "a83c3833-66a8-57a8-8cf8-f30d37c589b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({e}^{2st}+{e}^{-2st})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fd19d0f-60a8-5109-bf7a-caf1ec7c5da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358589} :answer-field/id #uuid "64926fc3-40d3-52da-bc14-bf84d37251ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360152}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2df77054-e089-51e6-a47e-2d85379912a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94747" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y},$ where $x={e}^{st}$ and $y={e}^{-st}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{st})^{2}}=-{e}^{-2st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{-st})^{2}}=-{e}^{2st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t{e}^{st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-t{e}^{-st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-2st})(t{e}^{st})+(-{e}^{2st})(-t{e}^{-st}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{st}-t{e}^{-st} \\\\  & =t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01632887-adf0-501e-9c55-0c43a8e3a195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357770} {:answer/id #uuid "0667969c-90fd-5446-9ffe-efe916cf6782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357809} {:answer/id #uuid "31a57553-0726-5177-afb7-c33ab426fd0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358192} {:answer/id #uuid "c24d7296-dc42-53b5-9df3-6afb6f82d279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359456} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7d37b8-0f46-5fbb-b1ee-35edde54b6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0667969c-90fd-5446-9ffe-efe916cf6782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357809} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae361e8-131f-5a9f-a75b-7ea0d6de8cc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360014}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "878a1123-ef9a-5a02-91a8-3dc4077906e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94749" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st).$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st))(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s{cos}^{2}⁡(st)-s{sin}^{2}⁡(st) \\\\  & =s({cos}^{2}⁡(st)-{sin}^{2}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c7ed61-be76-5c28-943e-73d839de469b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378040} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c7131-1236-565f-820c-71d0c9d6461f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378041} {:answer/id #uuid "81e6772a-0701-5fba-976e-107b1b9bdbb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2({e}^{2s}-{e}^{-2s})}{{e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378042} {:answer/id #uuid "96f1087d-82ca-53f2-b58c-87296e971578" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s}}{2({e}^{2s}-{e}^{-2s})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378043} {:answer/id #uuid "99ca07b6-ae62-53fa-8034-91167ff2ab00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6c7ed61-be76-5c28-943e-73d839de469b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378040} :answer-field/id #uuid "a12f54b9-7bd3-5a99-bf1e-a42a31564b96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378039}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b3dcc80-0abe-5373-bdc1-9e25975b2bb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94751" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $x=t{e}^{s}$ and $y=t{e}^{-s}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2(t{e}^{s})}{(t{e}^{s})^{2}+(t{e}^{-s})^{2}}=\\frac{2{e}^{s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2(t{e}^{-s})}{(t{e}^{s})^{2}+(t{e}^{-s})^{2}}=\\frac{2{e}^{-s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{-s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}\\cdot {e}^{s}+\\frac{2{e}^{-s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}\\cdot {e}^{-s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}+\\frac{2{e}^{-2s}}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})}{t({e}^{2s}+{e}^{-2s})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e42d90a-97cf-5414-b80d-82a4862b83de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s{cos}^{2}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385365} {:answer/id #uuid "016496f9-039d-53a6-96db-cf3960e6a193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385366} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6f4593-e298-5f84-b3e8-cd2d86629bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+4t{sin}^{2}⁡s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385367} {:answer/id #uuid "b99ddb71-4211-5aa0-a805-5281aabf9bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385368} {:answer/id #uuid "a78b674e-3f6a-5922-89c6-75ee6f89da98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-4{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "016496f9-039d-53a6-96db-cf3960e6a193" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2s{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385366} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c5d0091-d440-5a1e-b574-7e1631c61ea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385364}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66bc23d2-d72e-5919-9b3d-d85b675dd732" :question/math-academy-id "q-37201" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2},$ where $x=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡s) \\\\  & =2s{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡s \\\\  & =2s{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010a1725-78d9-5ef6-976d-66b572c1b87d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ts({t}^{2}-3ts+2{s}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385826} {:answer/id #uuid "3a12b2ee-d263-5ffd-96bf-c7a89479c594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{t}^{2}(s-2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385827} {:answer/id #uuid "cd01d3dd-a324-5561-83a5-466a5fba3616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({s}^{2}+s+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385828} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3eb310-caaa-5a1e-9eea-e12deb4203bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ts(1+ts)(1-st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385829} {:answer/id #uuid "39c971fc-9574-59c2-93dc-706cf5f69912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{s}^{2}(2s-3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39c971fc-9574-59c2-93dc-706cf5f69912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{s}^{2}(2s-3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385830} :answer-field/id #uuid "722f5fc1-8625-5de3-b976-ddcdf5183ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385825}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cddd99f6-007e-52c0-bac7-f496a002a8cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94748" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2},$ where $x=ts$ and $y=s-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(ts)(s-t)=2t{s}^{2}-2s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(ts)^{2}={t}^{2}{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2t{s}^{2}-2s{t}^{2})(s)+({t}^{2}{s}^{2})(-1) \\\\  & =2t{s}^{3}-2{s}^{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}{s}^{2} \\\\  & =2t{s}^{3}-3{t}^{2}{s}^{2} \\\\  & =t{s}^{2}(2s-3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}]}
